    "perfect-full": {
      "verifyPhase": "full",
      "includesAggregate": "perfect-fast",
      "performanceLast": true,
      "requireUnitExecutionCounts": true,
      "mirroredAggregatePrerequisite": {
        "target": "contract-gates",
        "reason": "perfect-full keeps listing the 21 gate names that contract-gates already reaches, so each of them is REACHED twice (directly, and again through contract-gates) inside one Make invocation. Make builds a target at most once per invocation, so they still EXECUTE exactly once -- the redundancy is deliberate because ~18 checker scripts assert their own target appears in perfect-full's LITERAL prerequisite list rather than following the DAG, and dropping the names would red every one of them. This declaration explains extra prerequisite EDGES only: a second execution is still a failure, an extra edge that does not route through contract-gates is still a failure, and if the mirroring ever stops happening the declaration itself reds."
      },
      "dualSurfaceTargetsRationale": "perfect-full reaches two proof surfaces: contract-gates as a Make prerequisite, and its own recipe's `node scripts/verify.mjs full`, whose plan spawns separate recursive Make invocations. Each target below is genuinely needed by BOTH surfaces and cannot be removed from either without weakening one of them. Every entry is held to exactly two executions and must still double -- if one stops doubling the gate fails and the declaration must be removed, so this records reality rather than muting the exact-once rule. Any target NOT listed here still fails on its second execution.",
      "dualSurfaceTargets": {
        "sdk-codegen-sync": "contract-gates needs a built wrapper and reaches this through operation-parity-drift -> mcp-tool-manifest-drift -> sdk-wrapper-build; the verify plan independently needs `make sdk-codegen`, which regenerates output/ts-sdk and wrapper/src and depends on the same sync step. Dropping the plan's codegen would stop perfect-full regenerating the SDK at all; dropping contract-gates' wrapper build would break the manifest gates.",
        "generated-edit-check": "contract-gates runs it plainly, to assert no guarded generated tree was hand-edited. The verify plan runs it inside the codegen group with CLOCKIFY_ALLOW_GENERATED_DIFF=1, where it guards the freshly regenerated tree instead. Different preconditions, so one run cannot stand in for the other.",
        "generator-comparison": "contract-gates runs it against the checked-in generated tree; the verify plan runs it after its own `make sdk-codegen`, i.e. against the tree it just regenerated. Collapsing them would stop comparing one of those two states.",
        "mcp-tool-manifest-drift-run": "reached by contract-gates via the operation-parity-drift chain and again by the verify plan's generated-contract group after the packages are built. The second run is what proves the manifest still matches a freshly built MCP server.",
        "mcp-write-safety-run": "same shape as mcp-tool-manifest-drift-run: contract-gates checks the committed state, the verify plan re-checks it against the freshly built server."
      },
      "requiredTargets": [
        "official-openapi-drift",
